Has the Court spelled out a positive obligation for the government? Typically, Article 11 imposes a positive duty to facilitate peaceful assembly, e.g., to grant permits, protect demonstrators, etc. In this case, the government granted permits for peaceful assembly in designated areas. The problem arises when the protest moved beyond the permitted area. The state may have a positive duty to ensure that the protest remains within the permitted area, but that's more of a duty to protect. However, the question asks: "If so, has it concluded that the defendant government violated this obligation?" The facts do not mention any ECtHR ruling on positive obligations. The case is presumably a hypothetical. There's no explicit mention of a positive obligation being identified. So we conclude no positive obligation violation.

